    '{"passage":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Vadim Alekseyevich Kozin (March 21, 1903 – December 19, 1994) was a Russian tenor, songwriter. Vadim Alekseyevich Kozin was born the son of a merchant in Saint Petersburg to Alexei Gavrilovich Kozin and Vera Ilinskaya in 1903. His mother was of Romani heritage and often sang in the local gypsy choir."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Their house was frequently full of musicians, exposing Vadim to tradition from an early age. He began to sing professionally in the 1920s, and gained success almost immediately. In the 1930s he moved to Moscow and began playing with the accompanist David Ashkenazi. Kozin was an openly homosexual man until 1934 when male homosexuality became a crime in USSR."}]}]},"keywords":[{"id":"kw1","text":"Romani"},{"id":"kw2","text":"1934"}],"difficulty":"easy","source":{"provider":"wikipedia","title":"Vadim Kozin","pageid":1530722,"revision_id":1347302638,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vadim_Kozin","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","retrieved_at":"2026-07-11T15:06:19.295Z"}}'::jsonb,
