    '{"passage":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ernst Krankemann (19 December 1895 – 28 July 1941) was an infamous Kapo in Auschwitz concentration camp. A German common criminal, he was transferred into Auschwitz on 29 August 1940, after being sentenced to life imprisonment on a murder conviction."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Although generally disliked amongst the Schutzstaffel guards, Krankemann had powerful supporters such as Karl Fritzsch, the camp''s lagerführer and second-in-charge to commandant Rudolf Hoess. As a Kapo, Krankemann held great power over other inmates of Auschwitz, including the authority to murder."}]}]},"keywords":[{"id":"kw1","text":"1940"},{"id":"kw2","text":"Hoess"}],"difficulty":"easy","source":{"provider":"wikipedia","title":"Ernst Krankemann","pageid":15545378,"revision_id":1325463237,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ernst_Krankemann","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","retrieved_at":"2026-07-11T15:06:19.295Z"}}'::jsonb,
