    'a1000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000001',
    '{"passage":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tudor Revival architecture, also known as mock Tudor in the UK, first manifested in domestic architecture in the United Kingdom in the latter half of the 19th century. Based on revival of aspects that were perceived as Tudor architecture, in reality it usually took the style of English vernacular architecture of the Middle Ages that had survived into the Tudor period."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The style later became an influence elsewhere, especially the British colonies. For example, in New Zealand, the architect Francis Petre adapted the style for the local climate. In Singapore, then a British colony, architects such as Regent Alfred John Bidwell pioneered what became known as the Black and White House."}]}]},"keywords":[{"id":"kw1","text":"Zealand"},{"id":"kw2","text":"British"},{"id":"kw3","text":"English"},{"id":"kw4","text":"Regent"}],"difficulty":"medium","source":{"provider":"wikipedia","title":"Tudor Revival architecture","pageid":2509553,"revision_id":1359057875,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tudor_Revival_architecture","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","retrieved_at":"2026-07-11T15:06:19.295Z"}}'::jsonb,
